    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Confirmation.Heading">Warning: Performance Impact</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Confirmation.Text" xml:space="preserve">If you choose to suppress this warning, MAA will use software rendering instead of hardware rendering, which can mitigate rendering-related issues but does not guarantee resolving all problems (such as dialog crashes). This will also cause:&#x0a;&#x0a;• Performance degradation&#x0a;• UI lag&#x0a;• Increased CPU usage&#x0a;&#x0a;Do you confirm to suppress the warning and accept these consequences?</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.ResetSuccess">Hardware rendering mode has been restored. Restart MAA to apply changes. Restart now?</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.ResetWarning">Software rendering mode is now enabled. Restart MAA to apply this setting.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.UseSoftwareRendering">Use Software Rendering</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.UseSoftwareRendering.Tip">When enabled, bad module warnings will be suppressed and software rendering mode will be used. This causes performance degradation, UI lag, and increased CPU usage. Uncheck to restore hardware rendering.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Warning.DoNotShowAgain">Do not show this warning again</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Warning.Epilog" xml:space="preserve">It is recommended to block injection at the system level via Windows Security: App &amp; browser control → Exploit protection → Exploit protection settings → Program settings → Add MAA.exe → Set "Disable extension points" to "Override system settings - On", then restart MAA.&#x0a;&#x0a;You may also try adding MAA to the exclusion list of the relevant software, or uninstall the related software and check if the problem persists.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Warning.Fallback">Additionally, the system's dialog component has been damaged and crashed — all subsequent dialogs may fail to display, and other system functionality may also be affected. Click "Yes" to suppress this warning, or "No" to dismiss for now.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Warning.Heading">Compatibility Warning</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="BadModules.Warning.Prolog">The following third-party software has injected DLL(s) into MAA, which will interfere with WPF rendering and ONNX/DML inference, causing MAA to crash, display rendering anomalies, or OCR recognition failures:</system:String>
